%[text] %[text:anchor:T_75128C09] # Carbon Neutral
%[text] [⇦ Return to the main menu](file:..\\..\\MainMenu.m)
%[text] The challenge of climate change encourages companies, such as MathWorks, to commit themselves to becoming carbon neutral. This effort leads to using renewable energy to power the company's facility. In this script, we will explore two sources of renewable energy: hydropower and wind turbines. We will apply the conservation of mass, momentum, and energy to understand how these renewable energy sources work and how much power they can generate.
%[text] 
%[text]{"align":"center"} ![Picture Hoover dam](text:image:0e3e)
%[text]{"align":"center"} Hoover Dam intake tower from Lake Mead, [USGS](https://www.usgs.gov/media/images/hoover-dam-intake-towers-panorama)
%[text]{"align":"center"} 
%%
%[text:tableOfContents]{"heading":"Table of Contents"}
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_9FF3671A] ## Energy in a fluid
%[text] 
%[text] In a fluid, energy refers to the ability of a fluid to produce work. It takes various forms:
%[text] - Kinetic energy refers to the energy of motion.
%[text] - Potential energy refers to the energy associated with external fields such as gravity.
%[text] - Internal energy refers to the molecular internal energy of the fluid. \
%[text] ![Try this icon](text:image:4494) **Try.** Following the prompt from `unitInfo`, explore all units of energy available.
unitInfo("Energy")
%[text] Just as the mass is often studied by considering the density (mass per unit of volume), we are often exploring the energy in the fluid by considering the specific energy  $e${"altText":"e"} (energy per unit of mass):
%[text]{"align":"center"} $e=\\frac{V^2}{2}+gz+u${"altText":"e=\frac{V^2}{2}+gz+u"}
%[text] Where $e${"altText":"e"} is the specific energy, $V${"altText":"V"} is the velocity, $g${"altText":"g"} is the local acceleration due to gravity, $z${"altText":"z"} is the elevation and $u${"altText":"u"} is the specific internal energy as defined in thermodynamics. You should check out the [thermodynamics module](https://www.mathworks.com/matlabcentral/fileexchange/126784-thermodynamics) to learn more about internal energy. The conservation of energy can be derived from the first law of thermodynamics:
%[text] ![warning](text:image:666d) This expression might look different from what you have seen in your textbook and lectures. **In this script, we use the letter** $V${"altText":"V"} **to denote velocities,** $z${"altText":"z"} **elevation, and** $u${"altText":"u"} **internal energy.** These letters are often used to represent other metrics. For example, V is commonly used for volume and  is also used for velocity.
%[text] From the first law of thermodynamics, we can derive the conservation of energy over the control volume:
%[text]{"align":"center"} %[text:anchor:M_F85E4F0C] $\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial t}\\int\_{\\Omega =CV} \\rho e\\; d\\Omega + \\int\_{\\Gamma=CS} (\\vec{V}\\cdot\\vec{n})\\rho e\\;d\\Gamma=\\dot{Q}-\\dot{W}${"altText":"\frac{\partial}{\partial t}\int\_{\Omega =CV} \rho e; d\Omega + \int\_{\Gamma=CS} (\vec{V}\cdot\vec{n})\rho e;d\Gamma=\dot{Q}-\dot{W}"}
%[text] Where $\\vec{n}${"altText":"\vec{n}"} is the normal vector to the surface, $\\rho${"altText":"\rho"} is the mass density, $e${"altText":"e"} is the specific energy, $\\vec{V}${"altText":"\vec{V}"} is the velocity vector, $\\dot{Q}${"altText":"\dot{Q}"} is the rate of heat exchanged with the surrounding, $\\dot{W}${"altText":"\dot{W}"} is the rate of exchanged work with the surrounding, $d\\Omega${"altText":"d\Omega"} and $d\\Gamma${"altText":"d\Gamma"} respectively represent the infinitesimal integration volumes and surfaces.
%[text] ![decorative](text:image:630e) **Pro-tip**. Dimensional analysis can help you verify what the term in the equation represents:
%[text]{"align":"center"} $\\int\_{\\Omega =CV} \\rho e\\; d\\Omega ${"altText":"\int\_{\Omega =CV} \rho e; d\Omega "}
%[text] The $\[\\;\\;\]${"altText":"\[;;\]"} operator is used to denote the units:
%[text]{"align":"center"} $\\left\[\\int\_{\\Omega =CV} \\rho e\\; d\\Omega \\right\] = \\left\[\\rho\\right\]\\left\[e\\right\]\\left\[d\\Omega\\right\]${"altText":"\left\[\int\_{\Omega =CV} \rho e; d\Omega \right\] = \left\[\rho\right\]\left\[e\right\]\left\[d\Omega\right\]"}
%[text] We know that:
%[text] - $\[\\rho\] = \\text{kg}\\cdot\\text{m}^{-3}${"altText":"\[\rho\] = \text{kg}\cdot\text{m}^{-3}"}
%[text] - $\[e\] = \\text{J}\\cdot\\text{kg}^{-1}${"altText":"\[e\] = \text{J}\cdot\text{kg}^{-1}"}
%[text] - $\[d\\Omega\]=\\text{m}^3${"altText":"\[d\Omega\]=\text{m}^3"} \
%[text] Therefore,
%[text]{"align":"center"} $\\left\[\\int\_{\\Omega =CV} \\rho e\\; d\\Omega \\right\] = \\text{kg}\\cdot{m}^{-3}\\cdot\\text{J}\\cdot\\text{kg}^{-1}\\cdot\\text{m}^3=\\text{J}${"altText":"\left\[\int\_{\Omega =CV} \rho e; d\Omega \right\] = \text{kg}\cdot{m}^{-3}\cdot\text{J}\cdot\text{kg}^{-1}\cdot\text{m}^3=\text{J}"}
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_F219A3F4] ## Power in a fluid
%[text] Power refers to the rate at which energy is transferred and can be expressed as energy per unit of time. In the [energy conservation equation](internal:M_F85E4F0C), the terms $\\dot{Q}${"altText":"\dot{Q}"} and $\\dot{W}${"altText":"\dot{W}"} are examples of powers. It is typically measured in units of energy per unit of time, such as Watts (W) or horsepower (hp).
%[text] ![Try this icon](text:image:2a61) **Try.** Following the prompt from `unitInfo`, explore all units of pressure available.
unitInfo("Power")
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_993C9C5A] ## Problem Statement
%[text] 
%[text]{"align":"center"} ![Picture data center](text:image:206e)
%[text] MathWorks pledged to become [carbon neutral by 2023](https://www.mathworks.com/company/aboutus/decarbonizing-mathworks.html). One of the major energy consumptions of its headquarters in Natick, MA, is a data center. In this script, we will study the possibility of offsetting this power consumption by generating renewable energy with wind turbines or hydropower. We will assume that the data center’s maximum power consumption is 1300 kW. 
%[text] ![warning](text:image:2b93) The data presented in this script are fictional and do not represent the actual power consumption by the MathWorks data center.
%[text] ![Try this icon](text:image:33df) **Try.** Load the data center electrical consumption data.
  %[control:button:414d]{"position":[1,2]}
load DataCenter.mat
dataCenter
%%
%[text] The electrical consumption from the data center is reported for every month of 2022, but what are those numbers referring to (energy? power?), and what unit are they expressing in?
%[text] ![Try this icon](text:image:7540) **Try.** Check the units used in this table.
  %[control:button:6a3f]{"position":[1,2]}

% Display the unit in registered in the table:
ElectricalConsumptonUnit = dataCenter.Properties.VariableUnits
% Transform the string representing the unit into a symbolic unit:
ElectricalConsumptonUnit = str2symunit(ElectricalConsumptonUnit);
%%
%[text] ![Try this icon](text:image:21e7) **Try.** Plot electrical consumption over the year.
  %[control:button:27ec]{"position":[1,2]}
bar(dataCenter.date,dataCenter.electricalConsumption)
ylabel(symunit2str(ElectricalConsumptonUnit))
title("Data Center Electrical Consumption")
%[text] 
%%
%[text] ![Exercise icon](text:image:3205) **Exercise.** Is the electrical consumption an energy measurement or a power measurement?
CheckElectricalConsumptionDimension(1) %[control:dropdown:6f79]{"position":[37,38]}
%%
%[text] ![Exercise icon](text:image:5294) **Exercise.** Calculate the total electrical energy consumed by the data center in 2022.
% Your code goes here:
TotalElectricalComsumption = sum(dataCenter.electricalConsumption);

% Display the results with the associated unit retrieved earlier:
TotalElectricalComsumption = TotalElectricalComsumption * ElectricalConsumptonUnit
  CheckAnnualEnergyConsumpion(TotalElectricalComsumption) %[control:button:13a5]{"position":[1,2]}
%[text] 
%[text] ![decorative](text:image:33d6) **Reflect**. As a comparison, a laptop consumes around 200 kWh per year.
%[text] 
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_D7217DD8] ## Scenario 1: wind turbines
%[text] 
%[text] We are now considering offsetting the energy consumption calculated in the previous section by installing wind turbines near Natick, MA’s headquarters. Wind turbines generate electricity by collecting energy from incoming wind and slowing it down.
%[text]{"align":"center"} %[text:anchor:M_EE0871FD] ![Illustration of wind turbine with aiflow going through it](text:image:2b77)
%[text] A power turbine with surface area $S\_T${"altText":"S\_T"}, generates power by collecting the kinetic energy from upstream airflow at velocity $V\_A${"altText":"V\_A"} (m/s). The airflow is slowed down and exits the system downstream at velocity $V\_B${"altText":"V\_B"}. The wind speed at the turbine is $V\_T${"altText":"V\_T"}. 
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_9A399D8B] ### **Control Volume Method**
%[text] 
%[text] This section applies the [control volume method](file:.\\ManOnTheMoonSoln.m) to the wind turbine. We are considering the control volume to include all the streamlines of fluid going through the turbine from the upstream disk A to the downstream disk B introduced in the [previous figure](internal:M_EE0871FD).
%[text] 
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_FA177D1E] #### **Mass Conservation**
%[text] 
%[text] All the mass flow coming in the control volume through surface A must exit the control volume through surface B. Therefore, we can write the following mass flow equilibrium:
%[text]{"align":"center"} %[text:anchor:M_A8D704EC] $\\rho S\_A V\_A = \\rho S\_B V\_B = \\rho S\_T V\_T = \\dot{m}${"altText":"\rho S\_A V\_A = \rho S\_B V\_B = \rho S\_T V\_T = \dot{m}"}
%[text] Where $\\rho${"altText":"\rho"} is the density,  $(S\_A,S\_B,S\_T)${"altText":"(S\_A,S\_B,S\_T)"} are the areas of surfaces A, B, and the area of the turbine, and $(V\_A,V\_B,V\_T)${"altText":"(V\_A,V\_B,V\_T)"} are the flow velocity through the surfaces A, B, and the turbine ($T${"altText":"T"}) itself.
%[text] 
%[text] ![Try this icon](text:image:4b54) **Try.** Define the problem parameters (velocities, areas) and compute the mass flow through the turbine.
  %[control:button:157b]{"position":[1,2]}
syms rho SurfaceA SurfaceB SurfaceT VelocityA VelocityB VelocityT
MassFlow = rho*SurfaceT*VelocityT
%[text] The variable `massFlow` represents the mass flow denoted $\\dot{m}${"altText":"\dot{m}"} in the previous equation.
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_FCEB9B1F] #### **Momentum Conservation**
%[text] 
%[text] The momentum conservation equation can be expressed as:
%[text]{"align":"center"} $\\underbrace{\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial t}\\int\_{\\Omega=CV}\\rho\\vec{V} \\; d\\Omega}\_\\text{Acceleration}\n+\n\\underbrace{\\int\_{d\\Gamma=CS}(\\vec{V}\\cdot\\vec{n})\\rho\\vec{V} \\; d\\Gamma}\_\\text{Thrust}\n=\n\\sum\\vec{F}${"altText":"\underbrace{\frac{\partial}{\partial t}\int\_{\Omega=CV}\rho\vec{V} ; d\Omega}\_\text{Acceleration} + \underbrace{\int\_{d\Gamma=CS}(\vec{V}\cdot\vec{n})\rho\vec{V} ; d\Gamma}\_\text{Thrust} = \sum\vec{F}"}
%[text] Where $\\rho${"altText":"\rho"} is the mass density, $\\vec{V}${"altText":"\vec{V}"} is the velocity vector and $\\vec{F}${"altText":"\vec{F}"} is the force applied to the control volume. We consider the flow to be steady, so the derivative with respect to time is equal to zero. There is no radial flow, so all the air entering the turbine upstream exits the turbine downstream. Therefore, we are left with the following:
%[text]{"align":"center"} $\\int\_\\text{\\Gamma=\\text{disk A}}(\\vec{V\_A}\\cdot\\vec{n\_A})\\rho\\vec{V\_A} \\; d\\Gamma\n+\n\\int\_\\text{\\Gamma=\\text{disk B}}(\\vec{V\_B}\\cdot\\vec{n\_B})\\rho\\vec{V\_B} \\; d\\Gamma\n=\n\\vec{F}\_\\text{turbine\\rightarrow air}${"altText":"\int\_\text{\Gamma=\text{disk A}}(\vec{V\_A}\cdot\vec{n\_A})\rho\vec{V\_A} ; d\Gamma + \int\_\text{\Gamma=\text{disk B}}(\vec{V\_B}\cdot\vec{n\_B})\rho\vec{V\_B} ; d\Gamma = \vec{F}\_\text{turbine\rightarrow air}"}
%[text] We are projecting the equation along $\\vec{e\_x}${"altText":"\vec{e\_x}"}, the basis vector along the horizontal axis oriented from left to right:
%[text]{"align":"center"} $\\int\_\\text{\\Gamma=\\text{disk A}}(\\vec{V\_A}\\cdot\\vec{n\_A})\\rho\\vec{V\_A}\\cdot\\vec{e\_x} \\; d\\Gamma\n+\n\\int\_\\text{\\Gamma=\\text{disk B}}(\\vec{V\_B}\\cdot\\vec{n\_B})\\rho\\vec{V\_B}\\cdot\\vec{e\_x} \\; d\\Gamma\n=\n\\vec{F}\_\\text{turbine\\rightarrow air}\\cdot\\vec{e\_x}${"altText":"\int\_\text{\Gamma=\text{disk A}}(\vec{V\_A}\cdot\vec{n\_A})\rho\vec{V\_A}\cdot\vec{e\_x} ; d\Gamma + \int\_\text{\Gamma=\text{disk B}}(\vec{V\_B}\cdot\vec{n\_B})\rho\vec{V\_B}\cdot\vec{e\_x} ; d\Gamma = \vec{F}\_\text{turbine\rightarrow air}\cdot\vec{e\_x}"}
%[text] Therefore,
%[text]{"align":"center"} $\\int\_\\text{\\Gamma=\\text{disk A}}(\\vec{V\_A}\\cdot\\vec{n\_A})\\rho V\_A \\; d\\Gamma\n+\n\\int\_\\text{\\Gamma = \\text{disk B}}(\\vec{V\_B}\\cdot\\vec{n\_B})\\rho V\_B \\; d\\Gamma\n=\nF\_\\text{turbine\\rightarrow air}${"altText":"\int\_\text{\Gamma=\text{disk A}}(\vec{V\_A}\cdot\vec{n\_A})\rho V\_A ; d\Gamma + \int\_\text{\Gamma = \text{disk B}}(\vec{V\_B}\cdot\vec{n\_B})\rho V\_B ; d\Gamma = F\_\text{turbine\rightarrow air}"}
%[text] Thus,
%[text]{"align":"center"} $-\\dot{m}V\_A+\\dot{m}V\_B=F\_\\text{turbine\\rightarrow air}=-F\_T${"altText":"-\dot{m}V\_A+\dot{m}V\_B=F\_\text{turbine\rightarrow air}=-F\_T"}
%[text] With $F\_T${"altText":"F\_T"} being the force of the force acting on the turbine.
%[text]{"align":"center"} 
%[text] ![Exercise icon](text:image:4f5b) **Exercise.** Using the variable `massFlow` corresponding the $\\dot{m}${"altText":"\dot{m}"} and the symbolic variable `V_A` and `V_B`, express the force acting on the turbine.
% Your code goes here:
ForceT = MassFlow*(VelocityA-VelocityB);

  CheckForceAirOnTurbine(ForceT) %[control:button:61c3]{"position":[1,2]}
%[text] 
%%
%[text] From the force acting on the turbine, we can compute the energy that is extracted from the wind using the following relation:
%[text] 
%[text]{"align":"center"} $dW = \\vec{F} \\cdot\\vec{d x}${"altText":"dW = \vec{F} \cdot\vec{d x}"}
%[text] Where $dW${"altText":"dW"} is the small amount of work that occurs over an instant $dt${"altText":"dt"}, $\\vec{F}${"altText":"\vec{F}"} is the force, and $\\vec{dx}${"altText":"\vec{dx}"} is air displacement during that instant $dt${"altText":"dt"}. The displacement $\\vec{dx}${"altText":"\vec{dx}"} can be expressed as:
%[text]{"align":"center"} $\\vec{dx} = \\vec{V}dt${"altText":"\vec{dx} = \vec{V}dt"}
%[text] Where $\\vec{V}${"altText":"\vec{V}"} is the velocity vector. In the case of our turbine, we can estimate that the small amount of work on the turbine  ($dW\_T${"altText":"dW\_T"}) during an instant $dt${"altText":"dt"} is:
%[text]{"align":"center"}  $dW\_T = F\_T \\cdot V\_Tdt${"altText":"dW\_T = F\_T \cdot V\_Tdt"}
%[text] Where $F\_T${"altText":"F\_T"} is the force acting on the turbine and $V\_T${"altText":"V\_T"} the velocity at the turbine. We can now estimate the power extracted by the turbine ($P\_T${"altText":"P\_T"}) as the derivative of the work with respect to time:
%[text]{"align":"center"} $P\_T=\\frac{dW\_T}{dt}${"altText":"P\_T=\frac{dW\_T}{dt}"}
%[text] 
%[text]{"align":"center"} %[text:anchor:M_44A13DD1] $P\_T=\\frac{F\_TV\_Tdt}{dt}=F\_TV\_T${"altText":"P\_T=\frac{F\_TV\_Tdt}{dt}=F\_TV\_T"}
%[text]{"align":"center"} 
%[text] ![Exercise icon](text:image:9139) **Exercise.** Compute the power collected by the turbine.
PowerT = ForceT*VelocityT;
  CheckPowerTurbine(PowerT) %[control:button:730f]{"position":[1,2]}
%[text] 
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_3D324D68] #### Energy conservation
%[text] 
%[text] Finally, we can consider the [energy conservation equation](internal:M_F85E4F0C):
%[text]{"align":"center"} $\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial t}\\int\_{\\Omega =CV} \\rho e\\; d\\Omega + \\int\_{\\Gamma=CS} (\\vec{V}\\cdot\\vec{n})\\rho e\\;d\\Gamma=\\dot{Q}-\\dot{W}${"altText":"\frac{\partial}{\partial t}\int\_{\Omega =CV} \rho e; d\Omega + \int\_{\Gamma=CS} (\vec{V}\cdot\vec{n})\rho e;d\Gamma=\dot{Q}-\dot{W}"}
%[text] Where, $e${"altText":"e"} is the specific energy, $\\dot{Q}${"altText":"\dot{Q}"} is the rate of thermal energy brought to the system and $\\dot{W}${"altText":"\dot{W}"} is the rate of work. Similarly to the previous calculation, the wind turbine is considered at steady state. Therefore, the derivative with respect to time is equal to zero. All of the fluid entering disk A exits through disk B and there is no radial flow, therefore:
%[text]{"align":"center"} $ \\int\_{\\Gamma=\\text{disk A}} (\\vec{V\_A}\\cdot\\vec{n})\\rho e\_A\\;d\\Gamma \n+\n \\int\_{\\Gamma=\\text{disk B}} (\\vec{V\_B}\\cdot\\vec{n})\\rho e\_B\\;d\\Gamma \n=-\\dot{W}\_{\\text{air}\\rightarrow\\text{turbine}}${"altText":" \int\_{\Gamma=\text{disk A}} (\vec{V\_A}\cdot\vec{n})\rho e\_A;d\Gamma  +  \int\_{\Gamma=\text{disk B}} (\vec{V\_B}\cdot\vec{n})\rho e\_B;d\Gamma  =-\dot{W}\_{\text{air}\rightarrow\text{turbine}}"}
%[text] We are using the same notation introduced earlier. The rate of work is equivalent to the power of the turbine $P\_T${"altText":"P\_T"}:
%[text]{"align":"center"} $-P\_T\n=\n \\int\_{\\Gamma=\\text{disk A}} (\\vec{V\_A}\\cdot\\vec{n})\\rho e\_A\\;d\\Gamma \n+\n \\int\_{\\Gamma=\\text{disk B}} (\\vec{V\_B}\\cdot\\vec{n})\\rho e\_B\\;d\\Gamma \n${"altText":"-P\_T =  \int\_{\Gamma=\text{disk A}} (\vec{V\_A}\cdot\vec{n})\rho e\_A;d\Gamma  +  \int\_{\Gamma=\text{disk B}} (\vec{V\_B}\cdot\vec{n})\rho e\_B;d\Gamma  "}
%[text]{"align":"center"} $P\_T\n=\n \\int\_{\\Gamma=\\text{disk A}} \\rho V\_A e\_A\\;d\\Gamma \n-\n \\int\_{\\Gamma=\\text{disk B}} \\rho V\_Be\_B\\;d\\Gamma \n${"altText":"P\_T =  \int\_{\Gamma=\text{disk A}} \rho V\_A e\_A;d\Gamma  -  \int\_{\Gamma=\text{disk B}} \rho V\_Be\_B;d\Gamma  "}
%[text] The specific energy of the air at disks A and B can be expressed as:
%[text] - $e\_A = \\frac{V\_A^2}{2}+gz\_a+u\_A${"altText":"e\_A = \frac{V\_A^2}{2}+gz\_a+u\_A"}    with $z\_A${"altText":"z\_A"} the elevation at disk A and $u\_A${"altText":"u\_A"} the air internal energy at disk A
%[text] - $e\_A = \\frac{V\_B^2}{2}+gz\_B+u\_B${"altText":"e\_A = \frac{V\_B^2}{2}+gz\_B+u\_B"}    with $z\_B${"altText":"z\_B"} the elevation at disk B and $u\_B${"altText":"u\_B"} the air internal energy at disk B \
%[text] The local variation of elevation in disks A and B is neglected ($z\_A = z\_B${"altText":"z\_A = z\_B"}). The air internal energy is considered equal at disks A and B ($u\_A = u\_B${"altText":"u\_A = u\_B"}). After integration we obtain:
%[text]{"align":"center"} $P\_T\n=\n\\dot{m}\\left(\\frac{V\_A^2}{2}+g z\_A + u\_A \\right)\n-\n\\dot{m}\\left(\\frac{V\_B^2}{2}+g z\_B + u\_B \\right)\n${"altText":"P\_T = \dot{m}\left(\frac{V\_A^2}{2}+g z\_A + u\_A \right) - \dot{m}\left(\frac{V\_B^2}{2}+g z\_B + u\_B \right) "}
%[text]{"align":"center"} $P\_T\n=\n\n\\dot{m}\\left(\\frac{V\_A^2}{2}+g z\_A + u\_A  - \\frac{V\_B^2}{2} - g z\_B - u\_B \\right)\n${"altText":"P\_T =  \dot{m}\left(\frac{V\_A^2}{2}+g z\_A + u\_A  - \frac{V\_B^2}{2} - g z\_B - u\_B \right) "}
%[text]{"align":"center"} $P\_T\n=\n\\frac{1}{2} \\dot{m} (V\_A^2-V\_B^2)\n${"altText":"P\_T = \frac{1}{2} \dot{m} (V\_A^2-V\_B^2) "}
%[text] This is a different expression than the one obtained [previously](internal:M_44A13DD1). However, both equations should be satisfied:
%[text]{"align":"center"} $F\_T V\_T\n=\n\\frac{1}{2} \\dot{m} (V\_A^2-V\_B^2)\n\\qquad (\\spadesuit)${"altText":"F\_T V\_T = \frac{1}{2} \dot{m} (V\_A^2-V\_B^2) \qquad (\spadesuit)"}
%[text]{"align":"center"} 
%[text] ![Exercise icon](text:image:2664) **Exercise.** Define the equation $(\\spadesuit)${"altText":"(\spadesuit)"} in the `powerEquality` variable using the `==` operator.
% Your code goes here:
PowerEquality = ForceT*VelocityT == 1/2*MassFlow*(VelocityA^2-VelocityB^2)

  %[control:button:4887]{"position":[1,2]}
if ~PowerEquality
    CheckPowerEquality(PowerEquality)
end
%%
%[text] 
%[text] ![Exercise icon](text:image:4bd7) **Exercise.** Solve the equation $(\\spadesuit)${"altText":"(\spadesuit)"} in order to express $V\_T${"altText":"V\_T"} as a function of the other parameters of the problem. Store the solutions of the equation in solV\_T
SolVelocityT = solve(PowerEquality,VelocityT)

 CheckVelocitySoln(SolVelocityT) %[control:button:8911]{"position":[1,2]}
%[text] ![decorative](text:image:18bd) **Reflect**. There are two solutions to the previous equation. However, physically only one should make sense.
%%
%[text] 
%[text] ![Exercise icon](text:image:8a41) **Exercise.** Substitute the right solution of the previous equation in the expression of the power extracted by the turbine `P_T`.
% Your code goes in the next line:
PowerT = subs(PowerT,VelocityT,SolVelocityT(2))

 CheckPowerTurbine2(PowerT) %[control:button:13c3]{"position":[1,2]}
%[text] 
%[text] The power collected by the turbine is now a function of the upstream and downstream velocity.
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_09866D42] ### Wind turbine efficiency
%[text] 
%[text] The efficiency can be expressed as the ratio between the power collected by the turbine over the maximum power available to the turbine $\\eta = \\frac{P\_T}{P\_\\text{max}}${"altText":"\eta = \frac{P\_T}{P\_\text{max}}"}. The maximum available to the turbine should be equal to the wind's power going to the turbine's surface if the turbine still needs to be built. This maximum power could be estimated using the following relation:
%[text]{"align":"center"} $P= \\rho S\\frac{V^3}{2}${"altText":"P= \rho S\frac{V^3}{2}"}
%[text] Where, $\\rho${"altText":"\rho"} is the mass density, $S${"altText":"S"} is the disk's surface, $V${"altText":"V"} is the wind speed. Now, we must select the right $S${"altText":"S"} and $V${"altText":"V"} out of the parameters of the problem: $S\_A${"altText":"S\_A"}, $S\_B${"altText":"S\_B"}, $S\_T${"altText":"S\_T"}, $V\_A${"altText":"V\_A"}, $V\_B${"altText":"V\_B"} and $V\_T${"altText":"V\_T"}.
%[text] 
%[text] ![Exercise icon](text:image:6f35) **Exercise.** Using the relation, we defined, calculated the maximum power, and saved the results in  `P_max`.
% Your code goes here:
PowerMax = 0.5*rho*SurfaceT*VelocityA^3
 
 CheckMaximumPower(PowerMax) %[control:button:01fe]{"position":[1,2]}
%%
%[text] 
%[text] ![Exercise icon](text:image:36cd) **Exercise.** Define the turbine's efficiency $\\eta${"altText":"\eta"} in `eta_T` as a function of `P_T` and `P_max`.
% Your code goes here:
etaT = PowerT/PowerMax;

 CheckTurbineEta(etaT) %[control:button:4d0a]{"position":[1,2]}
%%
%[text] 
%[text] The power efficiency of the wind turbine only depends on two parameters, the upstream velocity $(V\_A)${"altText":"(V\_A)"} and downstream velocity $(V\_B)${"altText":"(V\_B)"}. We introduced a single parameter $x = \\frac{V\_B}{V\_A}${"altText":"x = \frac{V\_B}{V\_A}"}. This parameter corresponds to how much the wind is slowed down by the wind turbine.
%[text] 
%[text] ![Try this icon](text:image:64e9) **Try.** Run the following lines of code to express the turbine’s efficiency as a function of the parameter $x${"altText":"x"}.
syms x
etaT = simplify(subs(etaT, VelocityB, x*VelocityA))
%[text] The expression of the turbine efficiency is now a function of a single parameter $x${"altText":"x"}. 
%[text] ![Try this icon](text:image:9482) **Try.** Plot the wind turbine efficiency as a function of the parameter $x${"altText":"x"}.
% Plotting function
fplot(100*x,100*etaT,[0 1])
xlabel("x (%)")
ylabel("\eta (%)")
title("Wind turbine efficiency")
%[text] 
%[text] ![decorative](text:image:79f2) **Reflect**. Does the wind turbine's efficiency have a maximum?
%[text] ![decorative](text:image:2f11) **Reflect**. Can you collect all the power available from the wind?
%[text] ![decorative](text:image:5083) **Reflect**. Why are we only plotting the solution with $x${"altText":"x"} from 0 to 100%? What would it mean to have a negative $x${"altText":"x"} or $x${"altText":"x"} greater than 100%?
%%
%[text] ![Try this icon](text:image:8a92) **Try.** Run the following code to determine the maximum efficiency of the turbine.
deta = diff(etaT,x);
xmax = solve(deta == 0,x);
if xmax~=0
    xmax = max(xmax);
end

eta_max = subs(etaT,x,xmax)
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_B5EBC7AA] ### Estimate impact of wind turbine farm
%[text] 
%[text] We just computed the maximum functioning efficiency of a wind turbine. We want to estimate how much energy could be collected in a specific location. We retrieve data from a [connected weather station](https://www.mathworks.com/matlabcentral/fileexchange/47049-analyzing-weather-data-from-an-arduino-based-weather-station?s_tid=srchtitle_weather%20station_1) in Natick, MA. Now that we know how much energy can be extracted by a wind turbine, $\\eta\_{\\text{max}}${"altText":"\eta\_{\text{max}}"}, we can use the Natick wind data to determine the energy a wind turbine could collect in this location.
%[text] Now that we know how much energy can be extracted by a wind turbine, we can use weather data from a weather station in Natick, MA, to determine the energy a wind turbine could collect in this location.
%[text] ![Try this icon](text:image:142c) **Try.** Load and visualize the weather data.
  %[control:button:618c]{"position":[1,2]}
load NatickWeather.mat
weather
%%
%[text] ![Try this icon](text:image:46b2) **Try.** Again, we can check the units in the timetable.
  %[control:button:082a]{"position":[1,2]}
weather.Properties.VariableUnits
%%
%[text] ![Try this icon](text:image:1621) **Try.** The air density at sea level is estimated to be 1.2 $\\text{kg}\\cdot\\text{m}^{-3}${"altText":"\text{kg}\cdot\text{m}^{-3}"}, and the diameter of the turbine rotor disk can be defined using the following slider.
units = symunit;
DiameterT = 65* units.m; %[control:slider:2dd1]{"position":[13,15]}
SurfaceT = pi*DiameterT^2/4;
rho = 1.2*units.kg/units.m^3;
%%
%[text] 
%[text] The maximum efficiency of a wind turbine computed in the previous section was below 60%. This efficiency does not account for the electrical and mechanical efficiency of the system. Most wind turbine function at lower efficiency than the ideal maximum efficiency we defined. For the rest of this script, we will consider the global efficiency of the system is  $\\eta = 40\\%${"altText":"\eta = 40%"}. The maximum power to be extracted from the wind can be estimated as follows:
%[text]{"align":"center"} $P\_{max}=\\frac{1}{2}\\rho S\_T V^3${"altText":"P\_{max}=\frac{1}{2}\rho S\_T V^3"}
%[text] and, 
%[text]{"align":"center"} $\\eta = \\frac{P\_\\text{T}}{P\_{max}}${"altText":"\eta = \frac{P\_\text{T}}{P\_{max}}"}
%[text] 
%[text] ![Exercise icon](text:image:44bc) **Exercise.** Complete the following edit field to compute the power extracted by the turbine.
% Define the efficiency:
eta = 0.4;

% Your code goes here:
weather.powerExtracted =  eta*1/2*rho*SurfaceT.*(weather.Wind*units.m/units.s).^3 %[control:editfield:2c09]{"position":[26,47]}
  CheckPowerExtractedTurbine(weather,DiameterT) %[control:button:1801]{"position":[1,2]}
%%
%[text] 
%[text] ![Try this icon](text:image:22a2) **Try.** Based on your previous answer, we can compute the energy generated each day, the annual energy production, and the number of wind turbines that would need to be installed to power the data center.
% Compute the daily and annual energy produced by the wind turbine
weather.dailyProduction = weather.powerExtracted * 1*units.day

% Sum over the daily production to compute the annual production
TurbineAnnualProduction = sum(weather.dailyProduction)
if TurbineAnnualProduction ~= 0

    % Rewrite this energy in more common energy unit, kWh
    TurbineAnnualProduction = unitConvert(TurbineAnnualProduction, units.kWh) %[control:dropdown:36c7]{"position":[68,77]}
    
    % Compute the number of wind turbine that would need to be installed
    NumberOfTurbine = TotalElectricalComsumption/TurbineAnnualProduction

end
%[text] 
%[text] ![decorative](text:image:79ae) **Reflect**. How acceptable would it be to install this number of wind turbines in a suburban area such as Natick, MA?
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_59F7B775] ## Scenario 2: Hydropower
%[text] 
%[text] The second option to power the MathWorks data center is to build a retention lake near the headquarters in Natick, MA. The water collected in this retention lake would be used to generate hydroelectricity. We are considering building a retention lake inspired by the [MATLAB logo](https://www.mathworks.com/help/matlab/visualize/creating-the-matlab-logo.html).
%[text] ![Try this icon](text:image:8fdd) **Try.** Visualize the design of the retention lake.
  %[control:button:1ef0]{"position":[1,2]}
PlotRetentionLake
%[text] 
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_9CE25996] ### Creating a mathematical model
%[text] 
%[text] To model the power generated over a year by this installation, we will apply the conservation of mass to the control volume that is the lake. 
%[text]{"align":"center"} $\\displaystyle\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial t}\\int\_{\\Omega=CV}\\rho \\; d\\Omega\n+\n\\int\_{\\Gamma=CS} (\\vec{V}\\cdot\\vec{n})\\rho \\; d\\Gamma \n= \n0${"altText":"\displaystyle\frac{\partial}{\partial t}\int\_{\Omega=CV}\rho ; d\Omega + \int\_{\Gamma=CS} (\vec{V}\cdot\vec{n})\rho ; d\Gamma  =  0"}
%[text]{"align":"center"} $\\displaystyle\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial t}\\int\_{\\Omega=CV}\\rho S\_\\text{lake} \\; dh \n+ \n\\int\_{\\Gamma=CS} (\\vec{V}\\cdot\\vec{n})\\rho \\; d\\Gamma\n= \n0${"altText":"\displaystyle\frac{\partial}{\partial t}\int\_{\Omega=CV}\rho S\_\text{lake} ; dh  +  \int\_{\Gamma=CS} (\vec{V}\cdot\vec{n})\rho ; d\Gamma =  0"}
%[text]{"align":"center"} $\\rho S\_\\text{lake}\\frac{dh}{dt} \n+\n\\int\_{\\Gamma=CS} (\\vec{V}\\cdot\\vec{n})\\rho \\; d\\Gamma = 0${"altText":"\rho S\_\text{lake}\frac{dh}{dt}  + \int\_{\Gamma=CS} (\vec{V}\cdot\vec{n})\rho ; d\Gamma = 0"}
%[text]{"align":"center"} $\\rho S\_\\text{lake}\\frac{dh}{dt} + \\dot{m}\_\\text{rain} + \\dot{m}\_\\text{evaporation} + \\dot{m}\_\\text{turbine} = 0${"altText":"\rho S\_\text{lake}\frac{dh}{dt} + \dot{m}\_\text{rain} + \dot{m}\_\text{evaporation} + \dot{m}\_\text{turbine} = 0"}
%[text] Where $CV${"altText":"CV"} is the volume of water of the lake and $CS${"altText":"CS"} limit of the retention lake, $h${"altText":"h"} is the water level of the lake, $S\_{lake}${"altText":"S\_{lake}"} is the area of the lake, $\\rho${"altText":"\rho"} is the mass density of water, ($\\dot{m}\_{rain}${"altText":"\dot{m}\_{rain}"}, $\\dot{m}\_{evaporation}${"altText":"\dot{m}\_{evaporation}"}, $\\dot{m}\_{turbine}${"altText":"\dot{m}\_{turbine}"}) represents the mass flow of water into the control volume from rain, evaporation and through the turbine. To ensure the sustainability of the water resource, we are considering that the retention lake should constantly keep the same water height, therefore   $\\frac{dh}{dt} = 0${"altText":"\frac{dh}{dt} = 0"}, and:
%[text]{"align":"center"} $\\dot{m}\_\\text{turbine} = - \\dot{m}\_\\text{rain} - \\dot{m}\_\\text{evaporation}${"altText":"\dot{m}\_\text{turbine} = - \dot{m}\_\text{rain} - \dot{m}\_\text{evaporation}"}
%[text] **Remark:** The different $\\dot{m}${"altText":"\dot{m}"} are positive if the flow is leaving the control volume (*i.e.,* the retention lake) and negative if the flow is entering the control volume. We can abandon that convention and consider all the mass flows as positive. Then the previous equation becomes:
%[text]{"align":"center"} $\\dot{m}\_\\text{turbine} =  \\dot{m}\_\\text{rain} - \\dot{m}\_\\text{evaporation}${"altText":"\dot{m}\_\text{turbine} =  \dot{m}\_\text{rain} - \dot{m}\_\text{evaporation}"}
%[text] This equation describes that the mass flows through the turbine equals the mass flow received from the rain minus the mass flow of water evaporated. We consider that the precipitation and evaporation mass flow only depend on the local weather and the dimensions of the retention lake. 
%[text] ![Try this icon](text:image:03a5) **Try.** Define the parameters of the problem `lakeLength` represents the length of the side of the lake, `surfaceLake` represents the surface of the lake, `rhoWater` is the density of water in the lake, `deltaH` is the elevation difference between the surface of the lake and the turbine, `bassinArea` represents the surface from which rainwater is captured and injected into the retention lake.
  %[control:button:79d2]{"position":[1,2]}
LengthLake = 100*units.m;
SurfaceLake = LengthLake^2;
SurfaceBassin = 1*units.km^2;
rho = 1000*units.kg/units.m^3;
DeltaH = 10*units.m; %[control:slider:4d41]{"position":[10,12]}
%[text] 
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_C8D1FE78] #### Modeling the rainfall
%[text] 
%[text] The water collected from the rainfall is modeled using real data from a custom weather station installed on one of the parking garages at the MathWorks Apple Hill campus in Natick, Massachusetts.
%[text] 
%[text] ![Try this icon](text:image:25c9) **Try.** Load and visualize the weather data.
  %[control:button:7557]{"position":[1,2]}
load NatickWeather.mat
weather
%[text] 
%%
%[text] In this table, the temperature is reported in degree Celsius, the wind in meters per second, the relative humidity is in percent, the rainfall is in millimeters, and the dew point is in degrees Celsius.
%[text] 
%[text] ![Exercise icon](text:image:3a5c) **Exercise.** Complete the following edit field to compute the rainfall mass as a function of the daily rainfall recorded and previously defined parameters.
% Your code goes here:
weather.dailyMassRain = rho*SurfaceBassin*weather.Rainfall*units.mm; %[control:editfield:097b]{"position":[25,42]}
weather.dailyMassRain = simplify(weather.dailyMassRain)

% Show the annual rainfall in Natick in term of mass:
AnnualMassRain = sum(weather.dailyMassRain)

  CheckAnnualRain(AnnualMassRain) %[control:button:73e1]{"position":[1,2]}
%[text] 
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_B554E001] #### Model the evaporation
%[text] 
%[text] The evaporation of the lake is a function of the temperature, wind speed and relative humidity of the air. 
%[text]{"align":"center"} $\\dot{m}\_\\text{evaporation} = (\\alpha+\\beta V\_\\text{wind})S\_\\text{lake}(W\_s-W)${"altText":"\dot{m}\_\text{evaporation} = (\alpha+\beta V\_\text{wind})S\_\text{lake}(W\_s-W)"}
%[text] - $\\alpha = 600\\;\\text{kg}\\cdot\\text{m}^{-2}${"altText":"\alpha = 600;\text{kg}\cdot\text{m}^{-2}"}
%[text] - $\\beta=455 \\;\\text{kg}\\cdot\\text{m}^{-3}\\cdot\\text{s}${"altText":"\beta=455 ;\text{kg}\cdot\text{m}^{-3}\cdot\text{s}"} \
%[text] $W${"altText":"W"} and $W\_s${"altText":"W\_s"} are the humidity ratio and the maximum humidity ratio defined by the following formula:
%[text]{"align":"center"} $W = 0.62198 \\frac{P\_w}{P-P\_w}${"altText":"W = 0.62198 \frac{P\_w}{P-P\_w}"}
%[text]{"align":"center"} $W\_s = 0.62198\\frac{P\_s}{P-P\_s}${"altText":"W\_s = 0.62198\frac{P\_s}{P-P\_s}"}
%[text] where $P${"altText":"P"} is the atmospheric pressure, considered to be equal to 101325 Pa, $P\_w${"altText":"P\_w"} is the partial pressure of water in the air, and $P\_s${"altText":"P\_s"} is the saturation pressure of water. The saturation pressure can be approximated using Antoine's equation:
%[text]{"align":"center"} $log\_{10}(P\_s) = A - \\frac{B}{C+T}${"altText":"log\_{10}(P\_s) = A - \frac{B}{C+T}"}
%[text] - $A=8.07131${"altText":"A=8.07131"}
%[text] - $B=1730.63${"altText":"B=1730.63"}
%[text] - $C=233.426${"altText":"C=233.426"}  \
%[text] When using Antoine's equation, one must be careful with the units as the temperature is in degrees Celsius, and the saturation pressure is in millimeters of mercury (mmHg).
%[text] 
%[text] ![Exercise icon](text:image:060c) **Exercise.** Compute the saturation pressure based on Antoine's equation; remember to include the units!
% Antoine's equation parameters:
A = 8.07131;
B = 1730.63;
C = 233.426;

% Your code goes here:
weather.saturationPressure = 10.^(A-B./(C+weather.Temp))

% Answer check/hints:
 CheckSaturationPressure(weather) %[control:button:4df1]{"position":[1,2]}
%[text] 
%%
%[text] The partial pressure of water in the air can be computed using the following relation:
%[text]{"align":"center"} $RH = \\frac{P\_w }{ P\_s}${"altText":"RH = \frac{P\_w }{ P\_s}"}
%[text] 
%[text] ![Exercise icon](text:image:3994) **Exercise.** Compute the water pressure in (mmHg).
% Your code goes here
weather.waterPressure = weather.RH.*weather.saturationPressure/100

% Answer check/hints:
 CheckWaterPressure(weather) %[control:button:80e2]{"position":[1,2]}
%[text] 
%%
%[text] Remember that  and  are the humidity ratio and the maximum humidity ratio defined by the following formula:
%[text]{"align":"center"} $W = 0.62198 \\frac{P\_w}{P-P\_w}${"altText":"W = 0.62198 \frac{P\_w}{P-P\_w}"}
%[text]{"align":"center"} $W\_s = 0.62198\\frac{P\_s}{P-P\_s}${"altText":"W\_s = 0.62198\frac{P\_s}{P-P\_s}"}
%[text] ![Try this icon](text:image:75aa) **Try.** Compute $W${"altText":"W"} and $W\_s${"altText":"W\_s"} are the humidity ratio and the maximum humidity ratio.
weather.W = simplify(0.62198*weather.waterPressure*units.mmHg./(101325*units.Pa-weather.waterPressure*units.mmHg));
weather.Ws = simplify(0.62198*weather.saturationPressure*units.mmHg./(101325*units.Pa-weather.waterPressure*units.mmHg))
%[text]{"align":"center"} 
%%
%[text] The mass flow of water lost due to evaporation can now be estimated using the following relation:
%[text]{"align":"center"} $\\dot{m}\_\\text{evaporation} = (25+19 V\_\\text{wind})S\_\\text{lake}(W\_s-W)${"altText":"\dot{m}\_\text{evaporation} = (25+19 V\_\text{wind})S\_\text{lake}(W\_s-W)"}
%[text] 
%[text] ![Try this icon](text:image:2743) **Try.** Compute the total energy that could be generated from this retention lake
weather.massEvaporation = units.kg/units.m^2*(600+455*weather.Wind).*SurfaceLake.*(weather.Ws-weather.W);
TotalWaterEvaporated = sum(weather.massEvaporation)
%[text] 
%%
%[text] ![Try this icon](text:image:93a5) **Try.** Compute the total mass of water available to generate power.
% Total water available to produce energy
TotalWaterAvailable = AnnualMassRain - TotalWaterEvaporated  
%[text] The maximum energy that can be collected by a turbine can be estimated as:
%[text]{"align":"center"} $E\_\\text{turbine} = mg\\Delta h${"altText":"E\_\text{turbine} = mg\Delta h"}
%[text] Where $E\_\\text{turbine}${"altText":"E\_\text{turbine}"} is the energy, $m${"altText":"m"} is the total mass of fluid displaced through the turbine, $g${"altText":"g"} is the acceleration due to gravity and $\\Delta h${"altText":"\Delta h"} is the difference of elevation between the free surface of the fluid and the turbine.
%[text] ![Try this icon](text:image:00bc) **Try.** Compute the total energy that could be generated from this retention lake.
g = 9.81 * units.m/units.s^2;
EnergyDam = TotalWaterAvailable * g * DeltaH;
EnergyDam = rewrite(EnergyDam, [units.kWh])
%[text] 
%[text] ![Try this icon](text:image:2c1b) **Try.** Compute the energy usage that the dam could cover.
% Ratio of the annual consumption that would be covered
DamCoverage = EnergyDam / TotalElectricalComsumption 
%[text] 
%[text] ![decorative](text:image:3175) **Reflect**. How acceptable would it be to create a retention lake near a residential area to only power a small fraction of the data center?
%[text] 
%%
